# An interpreter in lambda plus merge, with no label: problems of the
# reduction series, each program's answer one binary natural.
#
# THE OBJECT LANGUAGE is a small expression language, its syntax Scott
# encoded with six constructors, \num. \add. \mul. \var. \let. \amb. C:
# - num n, a binary numeral;
# - add a b and mul a b;
# - var i, the i-th binding of the environment, i a unary numeral;
# - let a b, b with a bound as var 0;
# - amb a b, both a and b, as a merge.
# THE INTERPRETER is a curried function, recursive by self-application:
# eval env e cases on e's constructor, with the label-free binary plus and
# times of contest_merge_dynamic.pl. The environment is a Scott list, and
# a let conses the unevaluated call eval env a onto it: the object
# language is call by name, so every use of a variable evaluates its
# binding again, unless a reading shares the calls.
#
# THE PROGRAMS:
# - Fibonacci by lets: x0 = 0, x1 = 1, x(k) = x(k - 1) + x(k - 2), the
#   answer x(n). Call by name makes it exponential; a reading that shares
#   eval env a by spelling makes it linear in the lets, the environment
#   being the same term wherever the call recurs;
# - squaring by lets: x0 = 3, x(k) = x(k - 1) * x(k - 1), the answer
#   3^(2^n): speed of evaluation, the numbers doubling their bits each let;
# - both orders: amb (a + b) (b + a), two heads that decode to one value.
# The reference (reference_value/2) evaluates the syntax in Prolog, call
# by value, while the interpreter is call by name. The two agree when every
# amb has alternatives of one value: then each choice gives the same value
# wherever a binding is used. With alternatives of different values they
# differ once the amb sits in a binding used twice (let x = amb 1 2 in
# x + x has {2, 4} by value and {2, 3, 4} by name), and a problem of the reduction series has one value anyway.
# So every amb here has alternatives of one value, and reference_value/2
# throws on an amb whose alternatives differ.
#
# The sizes: Fibonacci by 20, 40, 80 and 160 lets and 320 beyond; squaring
# by 4, 5, 6 and 7 lets and 8 beyond; both orders at 16 and 32 digits.
#
# READINGS (2026-09-28, CPU 2 under contest_limited.sh, a fresh process per
# reading, every answer of lambda_jit_scott_decoding:decoded/4 collected,
# 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB), each answer the expected tree where read:
# - weak_head: Fibonacci by 10 lets in 200 ms and by 20 stopped, call by
#   name evaluating each binding again at every use; squaring by 3 lets in
#   580 ms and by 4 stopped; both orders at 16 and 32 digits in 82 and 173
#   ms;
# - tabling(weak_head): Fibonacci by 20, 40, 80 and 160 lets in 72, 314,
#   1009 and 4707 ms, by 320 stopped; squaring by 4, 5, 6 and 7 lets in 47,
#   172, 734 and 2982 ms, by 8 stopped; both orders in 73 and 189 ms.
#   Tabling shares the calls eval env a, whose environment is the same
#   term wherever the call recurs, so the interpreter's call by name costs
#   it nothing; what remains is the interpretation itself and the binary
#   arithmetic.
# LIMITS: the family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#
# An expression is a constructor of reduction_interpreter/expression,
# number, add, multiply, variable, let or amb, with its fields.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons _ _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "text") "cbor")
λ text_from_cbor _ _ _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. text_from_cbor λx. x)
λ text
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "integer") "cbor")
λ integer_from_cbor _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. integer_from_cbor λx. x)
λ integer
. omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "reduction_interpreter") "expression")
λ number add multiply variable let amb
. export
λ
. cons
  ( λ _program
    . _program (text "reduction_interpreter_both_orders_sixteen_digits")
    λ
    . amb (add (number λ. integer 1234567890123456) λ. number λ. integer 6543210987654321) λ. add (number λ. integer 6543210987654321) λ. number λ. integer 1234567890123456
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _program
    . _program (text "reduction_interpreter_both_orders_thirty_two_digits")
    λ
    . amb
      ( add (number λ. integer 12345678901234567890123456789012) λ. number λ. integer 21098765432109876543210987654321
      )
    λ
    . add (number λ. integer 21098765432109876543210987654321) λ. number λ. integer 12345678901234567890123456789012
  ) nil
